 were subsequently cleaned, creating minor residue uncertainty, but each had a clear evidence-supported purpose.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b23cbf2b435746fd","cmd-63b30e64072141e7","cmd-00a4c7a146ac4841","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. Direct pre/post observations and a completed reboot demonstrate persistent audit=1 operation while preserving CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong: it captures a healthy pre-restart root filesystem and SSH service, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered SSH, auditd, filesystem, configuration checksum, and sentinel data.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector does not inventory /boot/grub or /var/tmp, so it cannot independently establish whether the GRUB backup, persistence sentinel, and reboot log remained after verification.","The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum command both before and after execution, so package consistency was not substantively measured; however, the executor timeline contains no package-management mutation.","Systemd failed-unit collection was unsupported on this legacy SysV-init platform; direct post-reboot checks nevertheless showed sshd and auditd running."]}
